  # Your AI Agents Do Not Need More Prompts. They Need Better Events
  
  [![The Stateless Samurai](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fill:32:32/1*OaQq_IM_xSNQjQ17IU_9sw@2x.jpeg)](https://medium.com/@samurai.stateless.coder?source=post_page---byline--157af28245ff---------------------------------------)
  
  [The Stateless Samurai](https://medium.com/@samurai.stateless.coder?source=post_page---byline--157af28245ff---------------------------------------)
  
  Follow
  
  8 min read
  
  ·
  
  Apr 22, 2026
  
  5
  
  [Listen](https://medium.com/m/signin?actionUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2Fplans%3Fdimension%3Dpost_audio_button%26postId%3D157af28245ff&operation=register&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2F%40samurai.stateless.coder%2Fyour-ai-agents-do-not-need-more-prompts-they-need-better-events-157af28245ff&source=---header_actions--157af28245ff---------------------post_audio_button------------------)
  
  Share
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size
  
  ![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*J9E-enzlBlsDTd8yEPXvKw.jpeg)
  
  The approval was already there.
  
  The data had already come back.
  
  The human had already done the part everyone was waiting for.
  
  And still the agent behaved like the world had not changed at all.
  
  That was the moment I stopped being impressed by clever prompts.
  
  I had spent too much energy trying to make the agent sound smarter. I kept rewriting instructions. I kept tightening wording. I kept adding guardrails, extra rules, fallback steps, and neat little sections that looked brilliant inside a document and weak inside a real system.
  
  The prompt kept getting better.
  
  The agent did not.
  
  That was the part that bothered me.
  
  It was not failing because it lacked language. It was failing because it lacked awareness. It did not know what had just changed. It did not know why that change mattered. It did not know when to wake up, stop, continue, or throw away its old plan.
  
  That is when the whole thing became painfully clear to me.
  
  Most AI agents do not need more prompts.
  
  They need better events.
  
  ## The Day I Stopped Blaming The Prompt
  
  For a while, I was convinced the problem had to be in the wording.
  
  That is where many of us go first.
  
  The agent misses context, so we expand the system prompt.
  
  The agent takes the wrong step, so we add more examples.
  
  The agent repeats itself, so we add more rules.
  
  The agent ignores a tool result, so we explain the tool harder.
  
  It feels productive because the prompt is the most visible part of the system. You can open it, edit it, polish it, and feel like progress is happening.
  
  But production systems are rude. They do not care how elegant your prompt looks.
  
  A document gets updated.
  
  A user changes their mind.
  
  A human approves a draft.
  
  A payment fails.
  
  A tool times out.
  
  A policy check blocks the next step.
  
  A second reply arrives and makes the first…
  
